[size=6][b]FAQ and Quick Start[/b][/size]

Welcome to Devotion. This mod watches how your character actually lives - who you fight, where you travel, who you help, how you pray - and lets the gods of Tamriel notice. Your faith grows out of your deeds, not out of a menu. This guide gets you playing in minutes and answers the questions new followers ask most.

If you want a deeper look at the system behind all this, see the How Devotion Works primer. For everything specific to your character's people, see your race guide (for example, the Nord Faith Guide or the Khajiit Faith Guide). This article is the fast on-ramp.

[size=5][b]Quick Start: Your First Hour[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*][b]Your race is chosen at character creation, and it decides your gods.[/b] A Nord, a Khajiit, and a Redguard do not worship the same way. The moment you pick your race, Devotion knows which gods can come to know you. You do not select a religion from a menu - your blood already points the way.
[/list]

[list=1]
[*][b]Make your starting choice, if your race has one.[/b] Some peoples are asked, early on, to set the shape of their faith: a pantheon, a sect, a path, a tradition, or a life-mode. You will see a short screen that explains the choice, then a second screen to confirm the path you picked. Take a moment with it - it colors which gods notice you and how. If your race has no such choice, you simply begin.
[/list]

[list=1]
[*][b]Just play in character.[/b] This is the whole secret. Fight, travel, help people, pray at altars, finish quests, live the life your character would live. Behind the scenes, "piety" (your standing with a god) rises for whichever gods approve of what you just did. A Nord who hunts under open sky pleases Kyne. A traveler who shows mercy pleases Stendarr. You do not have to chase this. It happens because you are playing.
[/list]

[list=1]
[*][b]Open the Devotion panel to see where you stand.[/b] You can open it any time with a hotkey you set in the mod's MCM menu (the Mod Configuration Menu, reached from the system/pause menu). The panel has a "Patron" view (your closest god), a "Today" view (what you have done lately), and a "Book of Days" journal (your history of faith). Short notification toasts will also pop up in the corner when something meaningful happens.
[/list]

[list=1]
[*][b]Keep your deeds varied.[/b] Doing the exact same thing over and over earns less and less. The gods value a faith that is lived broadly, not farmed. Mix it up and your piety climbs steadily.
[/list]

[list=1]
[*][b]When a god offers to claim you, decide.[/b] As your devotion deepens, a single god may reach out and offer to take you as their own. Accepting makes that god your patron and opens the path to the highest tier of favor. Declining keeps your worship broad. Both are valid ways to play - see the questions below for what each one means.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Frequently Asked Questions[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]How do I see my standing with the gods?[/b][/size]

Open the Devotion panel with the hotkey you set in the MCM menu. The "Patron" view shows your closest god and how far along you are. The "Today" view shows recent deeds. The "Book of Days" journal records your devotional history over time. The MCM menu itself also lists your standing, and short toasts appear in the corner when a god notices something or your rank changes.

Your rank with any single god climbs through these tiers, in order:

[list]
[*][b]None[/b] - the god does not yet know you.
[*][b]Seeker[/b] - reached at 25 piety. The god has begun to notice you.
[*][b]Devoted[/b] - reached at 50 piety. A real, committed bond.
[*][b]Champion[/b] - reached at 85 piety. The deepest tier, and it asks for focus.
[/list]

The piety bar in the panel fills from 0 to 85. Piety is tracked separately for every god, so you can be a Champion to one and a stranger to another at the same time.

[size=4][b]Why isn't my piety going up?[/b][/size]

The most common reason is repetition. Devotion has a built-in guard against grinding: repeating the exact same deed earns you less each time you do it. If you kill the same kind of creature, or pray at the same altar, again and again in a short span, the returns shrink fast.

The fix is variety. A god wants to see a faith that is actually lived - fighting, helping, traveling, praying, completing quests - not one deed farmed in a loop. Spread your devotional acts around and your piety will rise normally. There is also a gentle daily ceiling (a bit over four points per god per day), so you cannot rush a single god to the top in one sitting no matter what. This is by design: real devotion takes time.

[size=4][b]A god went quiet, or I got a "Rites thinning" message. What happened?[/b][/size]

That is [b]neglect[/b]. If you stop honoring a god you used to serve, your bond with them slowly cools, and Devotion tells you so - sometimes with a "Rites thinning" note in the panel, sometimes with a soft drop in standing. It is not a punishment so much as a truth: a faith you stop tending grows distant.

The cure is simple. Return to that god. Do the kinds of deeds they value, pray at their altar, live their way again, and the bond warms back up. For a Nord who has let Kyne's rites lapse, that means getting back out under the open sky. Each race feels neglect a little differently - your race guide explains the shape of it for your people.

[size=4][b]Can I worship more than one god at once?[/b][/size]

Yes, absolutely. Honoring several gods - broad worship - is a completely legitimate way to play, and it is how many characters live. You will earn blessings from the whole circle of gods your race honors.

There is one trade-off to know: broad worship caps you at the [b]Devoted[/b] tier. You can become a faithful, blessed servant of many gods, but to reach the very top - Champion - you have to commit to a single one. See the next question.

[size=4][b]How do I commit to a single god, or reach Champion?[/b][/size]

Champion is the reward for focus. To reach it, you commit to one god as your patron. This happens one of two ways:

[list]
[*]A god may [b]offer to claim you[/b] once your devotion to them runs deep enough. Accept the offer and they become your patron.
[*]A patron can also [b]emerge naturally[/b] if your deeds clearly favor one god above the rest over time.
[/list]

Once you have a single patron and keep serving them, your piety with that god can climb past Devoted all the way to Champion (85 piety), unlocking their strongest favor. As long as you stay broad and split your worship, you will rest comfortably at Devoted - which is still a strong, blessed place to be.

[size=4][b]Do I have to visit shrines, or does normal play count?[/b][/size]

Normal, in-character play is the heart of it. You do not need to make pilgrimages to earn favor - simply living the life your character would live earns piety with the gods who approve. That said, shrines and prayer absolutely help. Praying at an altar and visiting holy places are devotional acts in their own right, and they are a good way to nudge a specific god's standing or to make amends after neglect. Think of shrines as a tool, not a requirement.

[size=4][b]How long does it take to reach Champion?[/b][/size]

For most players, reaching Champion with a chosen patron takes roughly [b]30 to 45 days[/b] of in-game time at a normal pace - that is one or two devotional acts a day, just playing the game. If you focus hard and pursue your patron deliberately, you can get there in about [b]20 days[/b]. There is no way to rush it faster than that: the daily ceiling on piety means devotion is meant to be earned over a journey, not bought in an afternoon.

[size=4][b]What happens if I become a vampire or werewolf?[/b][/size]

Your faith feels it. Taking on vampirism or lycanthropy strains your bonds with the gods, and in some cases breaks them. How sharp the consequence is depends heavily on your race and which gods you serve - for some peoples it is a deep wound, for others a manageable tension, and for a rare few it even aligns with their path. Because this varies so much, the details live in your race guide. Check it before you take the bite or the beast-blood, so you know what your character stands to lose or gain.

[size=4][b]Does Devotion conflict with other mods, or overhauls like Requiem?[/b][/size]

Devotion respects your load order and is designed with major overhauls like Requiem in mind. One important rule: Devotion is a replacement religion system, so run it instead of another deity-worship or religion overhaul, not alongside one. Other kinds of overhauls - combat, survival, world, and so on - are fine. List-specific compatibility patches for big overhaul setups are in development, so if you run a heavy modlist, place Devotion sensibly in your load order and check the mod page's compatibility notes for your setup before starting a new save.

[size=4][b]How is this different from other religion mods?[/b][/size]

Two things set Devotion apart. First, [b]the gods react to how you actually play.[/b] Most religion mods give you a flat bonus for praying at a shrine; Devotion watches your real deeds - your battles, your mercy, your travels, your choices - and grants standing based on the life you lead. Second, [b]every race's faith is genuinely different.[/b] A Nord's path to Kyne, a Khajiit's road home under the moons, a Redguard's sect, an Orc's life-mode - these are not the same system with different names. Each people has its own gods, its own way of honoring them, and its own consequences for falling away. Your faith is meant to feel like yours.

[size=5][b]A Note on Blessings[/b][/size]

Note: these are current beta values and may be tuned before release.

As your standing with a god rises through Seeker, Devoted, and Champion, you earn lasting blessings that grow stronger at each tier. These are tuned to fit serious play. For example, a Nord who serves Kyne might see stamina recovery improve a little at Seeker (around +5%), more at Devoted (around +10% stamina recovery and +10% frost resistance), and reach a powerful capstone at Champion (around +20% stamina recovery and +25% frost resistance, plus a special signature effect unique to that god). Other gods favor other gifts entirely - one might harden your armor, another quicken your healing, another sharpen a weapon skill. The exact gifts depend on your race, your god, and your tier; your race guide lists them in full.

One rule applies to everyone: only [b]one temporary "favor" blessing[/b] can be active at a time across all your gods. These short-lived favors are special boons granted in the moment, distinct from the lasting tier blessings above - and you can only carry one of them at once. Choose which favor to call on when it matters.
